const e='<h2>The Stage Manager situation, briefly</h2>\n\n  <p>When Stage Manager arrived, external display support came with it, then got pulled back to M1-and-later iPads only. Everything below that line kept basic mirroring. Years later, that\'s still the shape of it:</p>\n\n  <div class="table-scroll">\n    <table>\n      <thead><tr><th>iPad</th><th>External display</th></tr></thead>\n      <tbody>\n        <tr><td>M-series iPad Pro / Air</td><td>Stage Manager: extended desktop, real windows</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>A-series iPad Pro (2018, 2020…)</td><td>Mirroring only</td></tr>\n        <tr><td>iPad Air (pre-M1), iPad, iPad mini</td><td>Mirroring only</td></tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n\n  <p>A 2020 iPad Pro is a fast machine with a USB-C port that can drive a 4K display. It just isn\'t allowed to put anything different on that display. That\'s a software line rather than a hardware one, which is what makes it irritating.</p>\n\n  <h2>Why mirroring looks wrong on a monitor</h2>\n\n  <p>An iPad is 4:3 and a monitor is 16:9. Mirroring hands the monitor a 4:3 picture and the monitor pillarboxes it, black down both sides, sometimes a third of the panel. Everything inside that box is then scaled for a tablet held at arm\'s length rather than a screen across a desk.</p>\n\n  <p>You can\'t fix it in iPadOS, because iPadOS isn\'t drawing for the monitor. It duplicates the tablet\'s own screen.</p>\n\n  <h2>The workaround: an app that draws its own desktop</h2>\n\n  <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> doesn\'t mirror. It takes over the external display and renders a desktop at the monitor\'s own resolution, so a 16:9 monitor gets a 16:9 desktop, edge to edge. The iPad\'s own 4:3 shape stops being relevant to what appears there.</p>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/desktop-light.webp" alt="A light-theme desktop with a file manager and a browser window on an external display." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>An external display rendered natively rather than mirrored.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>Because it draws its own environment, it never asks iPadOS for Stage Manager and doesn\'t care which chip you have. Any iPad that can output video gets the same result.</p>\n\n  <h2>What you get</h2>\n\n  <ul>\n    <li>Windows you can move, resize, snap to edges and put full screen, several at a time.</li>\n    <li>A desktop browser with tabs, a bookmarks bar, downloads, saved passwords, reader mode and a built-in ad blocker.</li>\n    <li>A file manager that mounts iCloud Drive folders, folders from the iPad itself, and external USB drives.</li>\n    <li>A full PDF viewer with annotation, plus Office file preview.</li>\n    <li>A Photos app with a full-screen slideshow.</li>\n    <li>Installable websites in the dock, each with its own icon and window.</li>\n  </ul>\n\n  <figure>\n    <img src="/content/images/desktop-multitasking.webp" alt="A file manager, a notes window and a browser window arranged on one external display." width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Multitasking that doesn\'t depend on Stage Manager.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <h2>Getting set up</h2>\n\n  <ol class="steps">\n    <li>Connect the iPad to the display. USB-C to HDMI, USB-C to USB-C, or a multiport adapter if you also want power and a USB port. AirPlay works too.</li>\n    <li>Open XeOS. It detects the display and runs through setup.</li>\n    <li>Pick the resolution the monitor reports and set content scale for your viewing distance.</li>\n    <li>Pair a Bluetooth mouse and keyboard, or use the iPad\'s screen as a trackpad.</li>\n  </ol>\n\n  <figure class="is-narrow">\n    <img src="/content/images/settings-display.webp" alt="The Display settings pane showing resolution options and a content scale control." width="1600" height="1077" loading="lazy" decoding="async">\n    <figcaption>Choose the monitor\'s own resolution. This is also what removes the black bars.</figcaption>\n  </figure>\n\n  <p>The full walkthrough is in <a href="/guides/connect-iphone-ipad-to-monitor-or-tv">the setup guide</a>.</p>\n\n  <h2>iPad-specific notes</h2>\n\n  <p>The window overview is <strong>Option ↑</strong> on iPad rather than Ctrl ↑, because iPadOS reserves the Ctrl combination. An iPad\'s larger screen also makes the screen-as-trackpad fallback comfortable enough that you can skip carrying a mouse if you want to. And a Magic Keyboard, or any Bluetooth keyboard, works normally: typing goes to whichever field is focused on the external display.</p>\n\n  <h2>What it isn\'t</h2>\n\n  <p>It\'s a desktop environment rather than iPadOS on a monitor. Your other iPad apps don\'t run inside it. What runs is the browser, the built-in apps, and any website you install. For a lot of work that\'s the whole job. If you specifically need Procreate or Final Cut on a big screen, this isn\'t that.</p>\n\n  <p>And some embedded page elements, consent banners, certain sign-in and payment frames, won\'t take a click from the external display, because iOS\'s browser engine hardens cross-domain frames against programmatic input. There\'s a one-tap workaround built in, described in <a href="/guides/device-layout-mode">the Device Layout Mode guide</a>.</p>\n\n  <div class="cta">\n    <p><strong>XeOS: External Display Browser</strong> gives any iPad a full-screen desktop on any monitor, no Stage Manager, no M-series chip required.</p>\n    <p><a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/external-display-browser/id6758286241" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="app-store-link">Download XeOS on the App Store</a></p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class="faq">\n    <h2>Frequently asked</h2>\n\n    <h3>Will this work on a 2018 iPad Pro?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. Anything that can output video to a display works.</p>\n\n    <h3>Does it need Stage Manager turned on?</h3>\n    <p>No, it doesn\'t use Stage Manager at all.</p>\n\n    <h3>Can I use it on an M-series iPad too?</h3>\n    <p>Yes. Some people prefer it there for the full-screen browser and the built-in ad blocker.</p>\n\n    <h3>Will the iPad charge while connected?</h3>\n    <p>Only if the connection carries power. That means a multiport adapter with pass-through charging, or a USB-C monitor that supplies power.</p>\n  </div>\n';export{e as default};
//# sourceMappingURL=ipad-external-display-without-stage-manager-B88D_6Y-.js.map
